<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Migraine: Best Points for Relief and Prevention" description="Learn how to use acupressure for migraine relief and prevention. Guide to GB20, LI4, LV3, and other points that reduce migraine frequency and intensity." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Migraine: Best Points for Relief and Prevention", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-migraine/" }]}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Migraine: Best Points for Relief and Prevention</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Acupressure offers a drug-free approach to managing migraine pain and reducing attack frequency through targeted point stimulation.</p><section id="gb20" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">1. GB20 (Fengchi) - Wind Pool</h2><p class="mb-3">GB20 is the most important point for migraine relief. It releases tension in the neck and skull base that often triggers or accompanies migraines.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the hollows at the base of the skull, between the two vertical neck muscles. <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/">GB20 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press upward into the skull base depressions with both thumbs simultaneously. Hold for 3-5 minutes.</p></section><section id="li4" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">2. LI4 (Hegu) - Joining Valley</h2><p class="mb-3">LI4 is effective for migraine pain, particularly for pain that is frontal or temporal. <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/">LI4 guide</a>.</p></section><section id="lv3" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">3. LV3 (Taichong) - Great Surge</h2><p class="mb-3">LV3 is especially indicated for migraines linked to stress, anger, or liver Qi stagnation. It helps redirect energy downward and calm the nervous system. <a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/">LV3 guide</a>.</p></section><section id="taiyang" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">4. Taiyang (Sun Point)</h2><p class="mb-3">This extra point is located in the temple region and provides direct relief for migraine pain concentrated at the sides of the head.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the depression at the temple, about one finger-width behind the midpoint between the outer eyebrow and eye corner.</p></section><section id="gb21" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">5. GB21 (Jianjing) - Shoulder Well</h2><p class="mb-3">GB21 releases shoulder tension that commonly contributes to migraine attacks.</p></section><section id="routine" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">During an Attack</h2><ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>Apply firm pressure to GB20 for 3-5 minutes</li><li>Press Taiyang points at both temples for 2 minutes</li><li>Stimulate LI4 on both hands for 2 minutes each</li><li>Finish with LV3 on both feet for 2 minutes each</li><li>Rest in a dark, quiet room for 20-30 minutes</li></ol></section><section id="prevention" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Preventive Daily Routine</h2><p class="mb-3">For reducing migraine frequency, practice this routine twice daily:</p><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><li>GB20 - 2 minutes each side</li><li>LI4 - 1 minute each hand</li><li>LV3 - 1 minute each foot</li><li>GB21 - 1 minute each shoulder</li></ul></section><section id="evidence" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Scientific Evidence</h2><p class="mb-3">A 2023 study published in the Journal of Headache and Pain found that daily acupressure reduced migraine frequency by 50% over 8 weeks. Participants also reported reduced pain intensity and less reliance on acute migraine medications.</p></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> Migraines require proper medical diagnosis. Acupressure is a complementary approach, not a replacement for prescribed treatments. Seek emergency care for sudden, severe headache (thunderclap headache).</p></div></article>
</BaseLayout>
